-- Seed: round 3 — the six payers that answered nothing.
--
-- Molina Ohio, Molina South Carolina, Medical Mutual of Ohio, CareSource
-- Ohio, First Choice by Select Health, and Aetna held no rule of any kind.
-- A practice billing them got "Unknown" at the point of care.
--
-- HOW THE DOCUMENTS WERE OBTAINED
-- Every one of these payers blocks automated access: Akamai 403 (Molina,
-- Aetna), HTTP 406 to any scripted request including a full Chrome header
-- set (CareSource), Imperva 403 (Select Health), and a connection timeout
-- (Medical Mutual). Two techniques got past all of them, and both are
-- worth keeping:
--   1. THE FILES ARE RARELY ON THE HOSTNAME THAT BLOCKS YOU.
--      es.aetna.com — the Spanish-language sibling host — serves the
--      identical asset paths as www.aetna.com with HTTP 200 and no bot
--      challenge. Molina's PDFs live under a Sitecore asset path that
--      keyword search never surfaces.
--   2. The Internet Archive mirrors these sites and serves them fine. Its
--      CDX index, queried by PATH PREFIX rather than by keyword, is what
--      found the current documents.
--
-- CONSOLIDATION. fetchPayerRule ends in LIMIT 1, so one live row per
-- (payer, state, code, attribute). The extraction put up to ELEVEN rules
-- on a single key — Molina Ohio states its out-of-network prior-auth rule
-- in three separate sections, for instance. Each is real and separately
-- quotable, so none is discarded: the most specific becomes the answer and
-- the citation, and the rest are carried in value->'supportingQuotes'
-- with their own verbatim sentences. 1474 statements were merged in this way.
--
-- LINE OF BUSINESS — the judgement call in this round.
-- Nine First Choice rules came from the First Choice NEXT provider manual,
-- whose every page reads "This document applies to First Choice Next
-- individual health insurance products for both on and off the exchange."
-- That is the ACA exchange line; the payer registered here is the SC
-- MEDICAID MCO. Those rules are DROPPED rather than relabelled — there is
-- no evidence the Medicaid line says the same thing, and a confident wrong
-- answer is worse than Unknown. Only the Select Health SC documents
-- survive for that payer.
--
-- The auditor separately rejected 19 rules, mostly for mapping a home
-- health AGENCY benefit onto physician home-visit E/M, for quoting a
-- definition rather than a rule, and for reading a network-access
-- statement as a prior-auth requirement.
--
-- Every quote re-verified verbatim and contiguous against text extracted
-- from the source PDF at build time. Anything unfound was dropped.
--
-- 620 rules over 620 keys, 11 source documents.
-- Idempotent: deterministic UUIDs + ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE.

BEGIN;

