  ('b7183b17-1d5d-4b7b-8690-1dc30bae3fd4'::uuid, 'a0000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000307'::uuid, 'OH', 'medicaid_mco', '99499', 'bundled_with', '{"answer":"Molina applies NCCI procedure-to-procedure bundling edits. An E/M service performed on the same day, by the same physician, in conjunction with a procedure bundles into that procedure with no separate reimbursement where the sole purpose of the visit was to perform the procedure. This is an explicit rule about E/M generally, so it applies to the home-visit E/M codes and the other E/M-family codes on this list. Providers must report the most comprehensive CPT code and append the most appropriate modifier when one is required to break an edit. Mapped by service (''an evaluation and management (E&M) code''); no specific codes named. Excludes G0179-G0181, which are not E/M visit codes.","appliesToService":"services/procedures performed in conjunction with an evaluation and management (E&M) code","mappedFrom":"service-level rule mapped to this code","sourceDocument":"Molina Healthcare of Ohio Medicaid — Provider Manual + Addendum (July 1, 2025)","supportingQuotes":[]}'::jsonb, 'varies', 0.75, DATE '2026-01-01', NULL, 'f1000000-0001-4000-8000-00000000a001'::uuid, 'Based on NCCI Coding Manual and CPT guidelines, some services/procedures performed in conjunction with an evaluation and management (E&M) code will bundle into the procedure when performed by the same physician, and separate reimbursement will not be allowed if the sole purpose for a visit is to perform the procedures.', 'extract:round3-six-payers-2026-08'),
  ('5870ba19-6724-42b5-8436-560e97a2d615'::uuid, 'a0000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000307'::uuid, 'OH', 'medicaid_mco', '99341', 'units_per_period_max', '{"answer":"(1) The only quantity cap the manual imposes on these services: a PER-DAY, per-provider, per-patient unit maximum drawn from the CMS MUE table, applied through Molina''s claims adjudication edits. Relevant to add-on units of 99417/G0318 and to repeat same-day units of 99497/99498. The manual does NOT publish the MUE values and does NOT state any per-month, per-year or per-benefit-period cap for any of these services. Mapped by service (all HCPCS/CPT codes subject to NCCI); no codes named. (2) Hierarchy rule for quantity limits: where an Ohio Medicaid state benefit limit is tighter than the federal MUE, the state limit governs. The manual adds that a professional organization''s more restrictive standard may be applied above both. Practical effect: the federal MUE is a ceiling, not a guarantee - an Ohio-specific limit on these services can cut below it, and this manual does not publish those state limits. Mapped by service (all codes subject to MUE); no codes named.","appliesToService":"Medically Unlikely Edits (MUEs) - the maximum number of units of service under most circumstances reportable by the same Provider for the same patient on the same date of service","mappedFrom":"service-level rule mapped to this code","sourceDocument":"Molina Healthcare of Ohio Medicaid — Provider Manual + Addendum (July 1, 2025)","supportingQuotes":[{"label":"a state benefit limit [that] is more stringent/restrictive than a federal MUE","quote":"In the event a state benefit limit is more stringent/restrictive than a federal MUE, Molina will apply the state benefit limit.","document":"Molina Healthcare of Ohio Medicaid — Provider Manual + Addendum (July 1, 2025)"}]}'::jsonb, 'varies', 0.75, DATE '2026-01-01', NULL, 'f1000000-0001-4000-8000-00000000a001'::uuid, 'NCCI editing also includes Medically Unlikely Edits (MUEs), which prevent payment for an inappropriate number/quantity of the same service on a single day. An MUE for an HCPCS/CPT code is the maximum number of units of service under most circumstances reportable by the same Provider for the same patient on the same date of service.', 'extract:round3-six-payers-2026-08'),
