  ('510d9f39-3015-4484-87d2-36dcc5a8a200'::uuid, 'a0000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000205'::uuid, 'SC', 'medicaid_mco', '99427', 'documentation_required', '{"answer":"(1) Every prior authorization request must carry clinical information sufficient to document medical necessity, including pertinent medical history (treatment, diagnostic tests, examination data) and, for expedited requests, the rationale for expedited processing. The same list also requires member demographics, referring and referred-to provider demographics with NPI, ICD-10 diagnoses, the requested service with all appropriate CPT/HCPCS codes, and the location where the service will be performed. (2) For any authorization request, Molina SC generally expects current (up to 6 months) patient history relevant to the requested service, a relevant physical examination addressing the problem, relevant lab/radiology results, relevant specialty consultation notes, and any other data specific to the request. Effective 02/01/2026. (3) Every note must carry the provider''s signature and credentials. The medical-record standards elsewhere in the handbook repeat this: all staff and provider notes are signed physically or electronically with either name or initials, and all entries are dated. (4) Provider documentation for any encounter must contain a treatment plan and progress notes (alongside the member''s name and date of service, NCCI-compliant coding, and ICD-10 coded to the highest specificity).","appliesToService":"Providers are encouraged to use the Molina prior authorization form provided on the Molina website. If using a different form, the prior authorization request must include the following information","mappedFrom":"service-level rule mapped to this code","sourceDocument":"Molina Healthcare of South Carolina — 2026 Medicaid Provider Manual","supportingQuotes":[{"label":"Information generally required to support authorization decision making","quote":"Current (up to 6 months), adequate patient history related to the requested services.","document":"Molina Healthcare of South Carolina Medicaid — Pre-Service Review Guide, effective 02/01/2026"},{"label":"For a complete and accurate medical record, all Provider documentation must","quote":"Have the Provider''s signature and credentials.","document":"Molina Healthcare of South Carolina — 2026 Medicaid Provider Manual"},{"label":"For a complete and accurate medical record, all Provider documentation must","quote":"Contain a treatment plan and progress notes.","document":"Molina Healthcare of South Carolina — 2026 Medicaid Provider Manual"}]}'::jsonb, 'varies', 0.75, DATE '2026-01-01', NULL, 'f1000000-0004-4000-8000-00000000a004'::uuid, 'Clinical information sufficient to document the medical necessity of the requested service', 'extract:round3-six-payers-2026-08'),
  ('3d1ce138-9085-4f39-888c-bc576b982799'::uuid, 'a0000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000205'::uuid, 'SC', 'medicaid_mco', '99341', 'telehealth_allowed', '{"answer":"(1) Telehealth claims must be submitted with the correct codes for the plan type per applicable billing guidelines; providers must follow CMS guidelines plus SC state-level requirements (Molina points to scdhhs.gov provider manuals). The handbook names no specific telehealth modifier or POS for these codes - the modifier/POS convention comes from the CMS and SCDHHS guidance it incorporates. (2) Telehealth is permitted as a delivery channel for covered services generally - it is a method of accessing covered services, not a separate benefit. Molina names no code list, so a covered service delivered via telehealth is billed as that service under applicable billing guidelines rather than as a distinct telehealth benefit. (3) A practitioner delivering telemedicine must be licensed both in the state where the practitioner is located and in the state where the member is located - i.e. SC licensure is required to serve a Molina SC member remotely from another state. Telemedicine Practitioners are also a credentialed practitioner type. (4) Telehealth may not be billed when the member and the participating provider are in the same physical location. Directly relevant to home-based practices: a clinician physically in the member''s home must bill the in-person service, not a telehealth encounter. (5) Telehealth/telemedicine services must be obtained from a participating provider; not all participating providers offer them. Combined with the out-of-network rule, a non-contracted provider delivering telehealth would additionally require approval.","appliesToService":"Telehealth Claims and billing","mappedFrom":"service-level rule mapped to this code","sourceDocument":"Molina Healthcare of South Carolina — 2026 Medicaid Provider Manual","supportingQuotes":[{"label":"Telehealth and Telemedicine services","quote":"Services are a method of accessing Covered Services, and not a separate benefit.","document":"Molina Healthcare of South Carolina — 2026 Medicaid Provider Manual"},{"label":"Telemedicine Practitioners","quote":"Telemedicine Practitioners are required to be licensed in the State where they are located and the State the Member is located.","document":"Molina Healthcare of South Carolina — 2026 Medicaid Provider Manual"},{"label":"Telehealth and Telemedicine services","quote":"Services are not permitted when the Member and Participating Provider are in the same physical location.","document":"Molina Healthcare of South Carolina — 2026 Medicaid Provider Manual"},{"label":"Telehealth and Telemedicine services","quote":"Services must be obtained from a Participating Provider.","document":"Molina Healthcare of South Carolina — 2026 Medicaid Provider Manual"}]}'::jsonb, 'varies', 0.75, DATE '2026-01-01', NULL, 'f1000000-0004-4000-8000-00000000a004'::uuid, 'All telehealth Claims for Molina Members must be submitted to Molina with correct codes for the plan type in accordance with applicable billing guidelines.', 'extract:round3-six-payers-2026-08'),
