  ('70f9fe20-9b2d-4f45-8654-2bb53ab07b15'::uuid, 'a0000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000301'::uuid, 'OH', 'commercial', '99348', 'prior_auth_required', '{"answer":"(1) Blanket rule, not code-specific. Aetna''s national participating-provider precertification list (Updated August 1, 2026) lists as a precertification-requiring item any service where coverage at an in-network benefit level is sought for an out-of-network provider or facility, unless the services are emergent. It applies to every service a home-based palliative practice bills — physician home/house-call E/M (99341-99342, 99344-99345, 99347-99350), ACP (99497/99498), prolonged service (99417/G0318), home health certification/supervision (G0179-G0181), TCM (99495/99496), CCM/PCM (99490, 99439, 99491, 99424-99427) and unlisted E/M (99499) — whenever the rendering clinician is out of network for that member''s plan. The list also notes ''Some plans have limited or no out-of-network benefits.'' The document does not name any of these CPT/HCPCS codes; it states the rule by service situation. (2) Authorisation-after-N-visits rule. After an initial consultation, an additional referral from the primary care physician is required when the specialty visits will exceed the number of visits initially authorized by the primary care physician (and also when the visits require an extension beyond the referral expiration date, when the specialist wants to provide services not originally requested, or refers to a second specialist). The manual adds that Aetna recommends the initial consultative referral be authorized for one visit except where more visits are predictably needed — so a serial home-visit palliative caseload will routinely need referral extensions. Applies in plans that require referrals; no codes are named. (3) Blanket authorisation rule of the referral type (Aetna''s own guide calls precertification and referral ''both authorization requests''). In benefits plans that require the issuance of referrals for specialist care, the primary care physician must issue a referral before the patient''s visit to the specialist, other than for direct-access services or emergency care, and the referral must be for covered benefits under the plan. A palliative/home-visit physician seen as a specialist under such a plan needs the PCP referral on file before the home E/M visit. Referral requirement varies by plan (Aetna Open Access plans and some others do not require referrals). No codes are named. (4) Scoped blanket rule: Aetna requires precertification before it covers any out-of-network follow-up care after an emergency, either inside or outside the Aetna service area; precertification may be obtained electronically or by calling the number on the member ID card. Directly relevant to a home-based palliative practice picking up post-ED follow-up (including post-discharge transitional care) for a member whose plan treats the practice as out of network. The quoted line is the contiguous run available in the two-column source; the sentence begins ''We require precertification''. No codes are named. (5) Blanket rule: precertification is required whether Aetna is the primary or the secondary payer — the quoted bullet reads in full ''We require precertification when we''re the primary or secondary payer.'' (the quote below is the contiguous run available in the two-column source). Important for palliative practices where Aetna sits behind Medicare or another primary plan: a required precertification is not waived by Aetna''s secondary position. The same section repeats it for Innovation Health. No codes are named. (6) Blanket submission-timing rule. Where precertification is required for a service, Aetna requires the request to be submitted at least two weeks in advance; requests may be submitted through the Availity provider portal or by EMR-generated electronic request. Not a rule specific to any of the mapped codes — it governs any precertification a home-based palliative practice must obtain (for example, when a visit is delivered out of network under rule 1). The document does not name any of these codes. (7) Blanket rule on the life of an approval: where member eligibility and plan coverage for the requested procedure or service have not changed, a precertification approval is valid for six months in all states, unless Aetna says otherwise in the precertification decision. Relevant to home-based palliative practices tracking authorisation expiry across a long episode of care. Stated by situation, not by code. (8) Blanket rule: referrals should not be retroactive; the quoted sentence continues ''deny payment for retroactive referrals.'' A referral obtained after the home visit has already occurred may have its payment adjusted or denied. Applies in plans that require referrals; no codes are named.","appliesToService":"Coverage at an in-network benefit level for out-of-network provider or facility unless services are emergent","mappedFrom":"service-level rule mapped to this code","sourceDocument":"Aetna — Participating Provider Precertification List (updated August 1, 2026)","supportingQuotes":[{"label":"Referral policies -- additional referrals after an initial consultation","quote":"If the specialty visits will exceed the number of visits","document":"Aetna — Office Manual for Health Care Professionals (23.20.801.1 L, 12/19)"},{"label":"Referral policies -- referral for specialist care","quote":"direct-access services or emergency care, the primary care physician must issue a referral prior to the patient''s","document":"Aetna — Office Manual for Health Care Professionals (23.20.801.1 L, 12/19)"},{"label":"Follow-up care after emergencies","quote":"before we cover any out-of-network follow-up care,","document":"Aetna — Office Manual for Health Care Professionals (23.20.801.1 L, 12/19)"},{"label":"General information -- precertification when Aetna is primary or secondary payer","quote":"We require precertification when we''re the","document":"Aetna — Participating Provider Precertification List (updated August 1, 2026)"},{"label":"Submitting precertification requests","quote":"You must submit precertification requests at least two weeks in advance.","document":"Aetna — Participating Provider Precertification List (updated August 1, 2026)"},{"label":"Coverage changes and updates (precertification approvals)","quote":"If member eligibility and plan coverage for the procedure or service you asked for hasn''t changed, precertification approvals are valid for six months. This is true for all states.","document":"Aetna — Participating Provider Precertification List (updated August 1, 2026)"},{"label":"Referral policies -- retroactive referrals","quote":"Referrals should not be retroactive. We may adjust or","document":"Aetna — Office Manual for Health Care Professionals (23.20.801.1 L, 12/19)"}]}'::jsonb, 'varies', 0.75, DATE '2026-01-01', NULL, 'f1000000-000c-4000-8000-00000000a00c'::uuid, 'Coverage at an in-network benefit level for out-of-network provider or facility unless services are emergent', 'extract:round3-six-payers-2026-08'),
